-- Passage wg12-narrative-writing-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8284e33b-58a0-5813-9a59-c685ea9b21d6', 'wg12-narrative-writing-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-narrative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Pawns First$q$, $q$(1) Last fall, the branch library near my apartment asked for volunteers to teach chess to children on Saturday mornings. (2) I signed up because I had played in tournaments since middle school and assumed teaching would be easy. (3) On the first Saturday, eleven kids between seven and ten years old sat in front of me, and within five minutes, two of them were using the knights as pretend horses.

(4) I had planned a lesson on opening strategy. (5) Nobody listened. (6) A boy named Tariq asked why pawns could only move forward, and a girl named Sofia Lindgren wanted to know if the king was "the weakest one or the most important one." (7) The library also offers knitting and coding classes on weekends. (8) I realized that I had never actually explained the rules to anyone; I had only played.

(9) The next week, I threw out my opening lesson. (10) We played a game using only pawns, and the first player to reach the other side won. (11) The kids shrieked whenever a pawn reached the last row. (12) Over the following weeks, we added one piece at a time: rooks, then bishops, then knights, and finally the queen and king.

(13) By December, Tariq had beaten me in a full game, though I suspect I was a little distracted. (14) That's how my volunteering went.$q$, 'draft')
